{"term":"غسل","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ghusl","category":"ritual_spiritual_practice","subcategory":"post-coital purification","concept":"islamic_ritual_purification","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":5,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The mandatory Islamic full-body ritual ablution required to restore ritual purity (ṭahāra) after sexual intercourse, ejaculation, or other states of major ritual impurity (janāba).","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"In Islamic jurisprudence ghusl is a prerequisite for prayer and Quran recitation after sexual activity; the concept fuses hygiene, ritual law, and sexuality in a way that has no secular English equivalent."}
{"term":"انتصاب","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"intiṣāb","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":15,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Modern Standard Arabic clinical term for penile erection; verbal noun from the root n-ṣ-b meaning to stand upright or erect.","english_term":"erection","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Polysemous root — انتصاب also appears in legal and construction registers (establishment of a body, erection of a structure); context disambiguates the sexual sense."}
{"term":"القضيب المنتصب","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-qaḍīb al-muntaṣib","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"erect_penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":20,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'the erect penis'; القضيب is the standard classical and medical Arabic term for the penis (lit. rod/staff) and المنتصب is its participial modifier meaning upright or erect.","english_term":"erect penis","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"القضيب alone is the standard anatomical term for penis in medical and classical texts; the full compound phrase appears in urology and sex-education material rather than colloquial speech."}
{"term":"تصلّب","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"taṣallub","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Verbal noun meaning hardening or rigidity; in sexual contexts denotes the stiffening of penile erectile tissue; primarily encountered in medical Arabic.","english_term":"penile rigidity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary everyday usage is تصلّب الشرايين (arteriosclerosis) and تصلّب متعدد (multiple sclerosis); the sexual sense requires contextual disambiguation and is found mainly in urology texts."}
{"term":"شدّة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"shidda","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":5,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":22,"recognition_pct_peak":40,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun primarily meaning intensity, severity, or hardness; applied in classical Arabic medical texts to describe the firmness or degree of penile erection.","english_term":"penile firmness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Not a current sexual vocabulary item in MSA; recognition as an erection descriptor is limited to readers of classical medical literature (Ibn Sīnā, al-Qānūn); everyday MSA meaning is hardship or severity."}
{"term":"تيبّس","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"tayyubs","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":35,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Verbal noun meaning becoming stiff or rigid; applied in clinical Arabic to describe penile tumescence through the metaphor of tissue hardening.","english_term":"rigidity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Standard medical usage is تيبّس المفاصل (joint stiffness) and تيبّس عضلي (muscle rigidity); as a sexual descriptor it appears in urology texts and is rarely used in everyday sexual speech."}
{"term":"صلابة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ṣalāba","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":38,"recognition_pct_peak":38,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning hardness or rigidity; in sexual and urological contexts refers to the quality of penile erection, used in clinical discussion of erectile function and firmness grading.","english_term":"penile rigidity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"High-frequency general vocabulary word (hardness of metals, moral resolve); its use as a sexual erection descriptor is confined to medical and classical texts and requires explicit context."}
{"term":"نشوة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"nashwa","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning ecstasy, euphoria, or pleasurable high; in sexual contexts euphemistically denotes orgasm or the peak state of erotic pleasure.","english_term":"ecstasy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"نشوة is also used for the high of intoxicants and musical rapture; the orgasm sense is fully context-dependent and remains euphemistic rather than clinical."}
{"term":"ذروة المتعة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"dhurwat al-mutʿa","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'peak of pleasure'; a literary periphrasis for orgasm combining ذروة (pinnacle) with المتعة (pleasure/enjoyment).","english_term":"peak of pleasure","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"المتعة can carry Shia-jurisprudential connotations (زواج المتعة, temporary marriage) but in this compound that reading is suppressed; found in literary fiction and sex-education translations."}
{"term":"أوج اللذة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"awj al-ladhdhа","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":50,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'zenith of pleasure'; a formal or literary circumlocution for orgasm using أوج (zenith, originally astronomical) as metaphor for sexual climax.","english_term":"climax","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"أوج is borrowed from astronomical vocabulary (apogee/zenith); the phrase is more elevated and poetic than ذروة المتعة; found in literary fiction and translated clinical material."}
{"term":"قمة اللذة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"qimmat al-ladhdhа","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'summit of pleasure'; a literary periphrasis for orgasm using قمة (mountain summit) as a spatial metaphor for sexual climax.","english_term":"climax","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"قمة also means political summit (قمة عربية, Arab summit); the orgasm reading is entirely context-dependent and the phrase is found in literary and journalistic registers."}
{"term":"ذروة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"dhurwa","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning peak or pinnacle; functions as a common Arabic euphemism for orgasm when used in sexual discourse, parallel to English 'climax'.","english_term":"climax","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"ذروة is a high-frequency general vocabulary word; it operates as an orgasm euphemism the same way English 'climax' does — the sexual meaning is entirely derived from context."}
{"term":"بلوغ اللذة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"bulūgh al-ladhdhа","category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'attaining pleasure'; a formal circumlocution for orgasm framing it as the achievement or reaching of pleasure.","english_term":"reaching climax","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"بلوغ alone most commonly means puberty (بلوغ الحلم); the genitive construction بلوغ اللذة forces the orgasm reading via اللذة; found in sex-education texts and clinical translations."}
{"term":"إثارة جنسية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ithāra jinsiyya","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":70,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'sexual arousal' or 'sexual excitation'; the standard clinical and sex-education MSA term for the physiological and psychological state of being sexually aroused.","english_term":"sexual arousal","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The default term in Arabic health and psychology texts; جنسية (sexual) is a technical adjective derived from جنس (sex/gender/kind); widely used in translated medical literature and public health campaigns."}
{"term":"هياج","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"hiyāj","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual excitement","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":45,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning agitation, frenzy, or excitement; in sexual contexts denotes intense, often uncontrolled sexual arousal; shares a root with terms for rutting or animals in heat, lending animalistic overtones.","english_term":"horniness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"هياج is used for both human and animal sexual excitement; هياج الحيوانات (animals in rut) is common; the human sexual usage carries connotations of loss of control absent from the more neutral إثارة جنسية."}
{"term":"غُلمة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ghulma","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Arabic noun for intense, consuming sexual desire or lust; appears in medieval literary, juridical, and medical texts describing overwhelming sexual appetite.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Probably connected to root غ-ل-م (ghulām, young man/servant), suggesting vigorous youthful desire; found in classical texts including Kitāb al-Aghānī and Ibn Ḥazm's Ṭawq al-Ḥamāma; rare in modern speech."}
{"term":"شبق","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"shabaq","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning intense sexual desire or libido; one of the most common and direct Arabic words for sexual appetite, used across registers from literary to colloquial.","english_term":"horniness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"شبق is the most semantically direct Arabic equivalent of English 'horniness'; the adjective مشبوق is used colloquially; appears in both classical and modern literary Arabic without being purely clinical."}
{"term":"وخز الشهوة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"wakhz al-shahwa","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual craving","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":38,"recognition_pct_peak":38,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'prick or sting of desire'; a literary expression for the sharp, sudden onset of sexual craving, evoking desire as a physical pang.","english_term":"pang of desire","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"وخز means a prick, jab, or sting (وخز الإبرة = needle prick); الشهوة = desire/lust; the compound is a rhetorical phrase rather than a fixed lexical item and is not standard in everyday speech."}
{"term":"نزوة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"nazwa","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual impulse","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning whim, caprice, or sudden impulse; in sexual contexts denotes a spontaneous, impulsive sexual urge, often fleeting rather than sustained.","english_term":"sexual whim","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"نزوة covers both non-sexual caprice and sexual impulse; the fleeting, impulsive quality distinguishes it from the sustained desire implied by شبق or شهوة; also used for mood swings and eccentricities."}
{"term":"شهوة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"shahwa","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":40,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The primary Arabic noun for appetite, desire, or lust; in sexual contexts it is the default word for sexual desire, used across all registers from Quranic commentary to everyday colloquial speech.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"شهوة appears in the Quran (3:14, زُيِّنَ لِلنَّاسِ حُبُّ الشَّهَوَاتِ) with a semantic range from appetite to lust; it is the single highest-frequency Arabic word for sexual desire and arguably among the most culturally loaded terms in the entire lexicon."}
{"term":"رغبة جنسية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"raghba jinsiyya","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual desire","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":60,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'sexual desire'; the standard formal or clinical Arabic phrase for sexual appetite or libido, used in psychology, medicine, and health journalism.","english_term":"sexual desire","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"رغبة (desire/longing) + جنسية (sexual); more formal and neutral than شبق or شهوة; the standard term in translated psychology manuals and clinical contexts."}
{"term":"غريزة جنسية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"gharīza jinsiyya","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual instinct","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":70,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun phrase meaning 'sexual instinct'; describes the innate biological drive toward sexual activity, used in psychological and sociological discussions of human sexuality.","english_term":"sexual instinct","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"غريزة (instinct) is the standard Arabic translation of Freudian Trieb and Darwinian instinct concepts; غريزة جنسية parallels libido in clinical framing and is more instinct-focused than the desire-centered شهوة or رغبة."}
{"term":"وله","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"walah","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"passionate longing","concept":"amorous_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical noun meaning consuming passion, infatuation, or deranging longing; in romantic and erotic poetry denotes an overwhelming love or desire that unmoors its bearer.","english_term":"infatuation","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"وله is a central concept in classical Arabic love poetry (ghazal tradition); the state is often framed as adjacent to madness (جنون العشق); recognizable to educated modern speakers though not in active everyday colloquial use."}
{"term":"صبابة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ṣabāba","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"yearning","concept":"amorous_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":33,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical noun for intense pining, romantic or erotic longing; describes the aching, mournful quality of desire when the beloved is absent or unattainable.","english_term":"yearning","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"صبابة is found throughout classical Arabic poetry and the adab literary tradition; less widely known than وله among modern speakers; the connotation of aching absence and mournfulness distinguishes it from the more consuming frenzy of وله."}
{"term":"هوى","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"hawā","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"passion","concept":"amorous_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun meaning passion, desire, love, or inclination; one of the most fundamental and high-frequency Arabic words for desire, spanning from simple preference to consuming romantic and sexual passion.","english_term":"passion","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"هوى appears in the Quran (53:3, 79:40) with the sense of base caprice or self-indulgent desire; in classical love poetry it is the central term for consuming passion; colloquially it covers preferences of all kinds, making the sexual force entirely context-dependent."}
